<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lotharingia</h1><p class="st-meta">855 CE – 900 CE · capital Aachen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Northern share of Middle Francia, named after its king Lothair II, whose name survives in Lorraine.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum">Treaty of Prüm</a> in 855 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> left the northern part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. The kingdom took its name from him as the realm of Lothair.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It held the old Carolingian heartland around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a>, with wealthy abbeys and episcopal cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II's</a> reign was dominated by his attempt to divorce his childless wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutberga">Teutberga</a> and marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waldrada">Waldrada</a>, which Pope Nicholas I blocked, leaving him without a legitimate heir.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> died in 869 his uncles divided the kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Meerssen</a> in 870, and in 880 all of it passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a> made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwentibold">Zwentibold</a> king in 895, but he was killed in 900, and the land became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Duchy of Lotharingia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lotharingia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lotharingia" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Furfur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vertrag_von_Pr%C3%BCm.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>855 CE – 900 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aachen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lothair II, Zwentibold</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Frankish, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lotharingia, Regnum Lotharii</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=878&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lotharingia" aria-label="Kingdom of Lotharingia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lotharingia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5139 672L5139 672M5139 672L5199 691M5139 672L5139 672"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139 672h0M5199 691h0M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aachen. <a href="/atlas?year=878&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lotharingia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
